{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at the age of 12 after his father's assassination.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond III was born in 1140 to Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was the Count of Tripoli and his mother was the sister of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond III was the grandson of William-Jordan the Count of Tripoli and Cecile of France. He was also the great-grandson of Raymond of Toulouse.\n\nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli after his father's assassination in 1152. He was only 12 years old at the time and a regent was appointed to govern the county until he came of age. The regent was Raymond's mother Hodierna who governed the county until 1155.\n\n*The War with Nur ad-Din*\nRaymond III played a significant role in the war with Nur ad-Din the ruler of Aleppo and Damascus. In 1163 Raymond III allied himself with King Amalric I of Jerusalem and the two launched a campaign against Nur ad-Din. The campaign was unsuccessful and Raymond III was forced to sign a treaty with Nur ad-Din.\n\nIn 1167 Raymond III allied himself with King Amalric I again and the two launched another campaign against Nur ad-Din. The campaign was again unsuccessful and Raymond III was forced to sign another treaty with Nur ad-Din.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Imprisonment*\nIn 1187 Raymond III was defeated by Saladin at the Battle of Hattin. He was taken prisoner and imprisoned in Aleppo. Raymond III was later released from prison and he returned to Tripoli. He died in 1187 at the age of 47.\n\nRaymond III was married to Eschiva of Bures and the two had no children. He was succeeded by his cousin Bohemond IV who became the Count of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.00413406